const ARTS = [
  { id: 'a1', pub: 'The Ledger Review', by: 'Hana Okoye', date: '28 Sep', saved: 1, tags: ['cities', 'work'], title: 'The quiet economics of the corner shop',
    dek: 'Margins are thin, hours are long, and the shops keep opening. The arithmetic behind the most stubborn business on the street.', p: [
    'Ask a corner shop owner what they sell and the answer is rarely milk or bread. It is time. The shop is open when the supermarket is shut, it is two minutes from your door instead of twenty, and it will sell you one onion without making you feel strange about it. Every price on the shelf carries a small charge for that convenience, and most customers pay it without noticing.',
    'The hours tell the same story. A shop that opens at seven and closes at eleven trades for sixteen hours a day, and the busiest of them are the ones when every other shop on the street is dark. The late-evening hour, when people realise there is no milk for the morning, can be worth more than a whole quiet afternoon.',
    'The margins are thinner than they look. On a typical basket the shop keeps around a quarter of what you spend, but rent, power for the fridges and card fees take most of that before any wages are counted. Owners make it work by doing the wages themselves. The family that runs the shop is the shift pattern.',
    'What has changed in the last decade is the back office. Wholesalers now take orders on a phone at midnight and deliver at six. Stock that once sat in a cellar for a month turns over in a week, which frees cash that used to be locked up in tins of soup nobody wanted.',
    'Owners also lean on things that are not on the shelves at all. Parcel collection, bill payments and lottery tickets earn a few pence each, but they bring people through the door, and people who come in for a parcel rarely leave with only a parcel.',
    'The most profitable shelf is usually the one nobody planned: the small, odd selection that reflects who lives nearby. A shop near a hospital sells phone chargers and good chocolate. A shop by a school sells exercise books in September and nothing but ice lollies in June.',
    'None of this makes the corner shop easy. But it explains why, when one closes, another so often opens in the same unit within a year. The arithmetic is hard, and it still adds up.'] },
  { id: 'a2', pub: 'Salt & Iron', by: 'Pieter Vos', date: '26 Sep', saved: 2, tags: ['food'], prog: .42, title: 'Why every good kitchen has one bad knife',
    dek: 'The cheap serrated knife in the drawer is not a failure of taste. It may be the most honest tool in the room.', p: [
    'Open the drawer in any serious cook’s kitchen and somewhere under the good blades there is a knife that cost almost nothing. Its handle is plastic, its edge is serrated, and it has outlived three expensive chef’s knives. It is the one that gets used.',
    'The bad knife does the jobs nobody wants to spoil a good edge on: cutting string, opening boxes, sawing through a crusty loaf on a board that has seen better days. Because nobody worries about it, it goes everywhere, including the picnic and the camping trip.',
    'Serrations hide wear. A straight edge dulls and you feel it on the first tomato; a serrated one keeps biting for years because the points do the work while the valleys stay sharp. It will never be precise, but it is always ready.',
    'There is a lesson in it for anyone who buys tools. Spend on the things that need care and reward it. Keep one cheap, sturdy thing for everything else, and stop feeling guilty about how much you like it.'] },
  { id: 'a3', pub: 'Field Quarterly', by: 'Marisol Ferraz', date: '24 Sep', saved: 3, tags: ['food', 'work'], title: 'How the night shift built the modern bakery',
    dek: 'Long, cold fermentation is praised for its flavour. It began as a way for bakers to get some sleep.', p: [
    'Bread is made while the city sleeps because people want it warm when they wake. That simple demand shaped the bakery more than any oven or recipe. For most of the last century, the baker’s day began at two in the morning and ended before lunch.',
    'The slow overnight rise that bakers now praise began as a way to rest. Dough mixed in the evening and left in a cool room would be ready to shape at dawn. Nobody called it slow fermentation then; they called it a few hours in bed.',
    'Refrigeration changed the clock. With a retarder, a baker can shape loaves in the afternoon, hold them overnight at four degrees and bake straight from cold at six. The bread is better, and the baker sees daylight.',
    'The schedule also explains the shape of the shop. Bakeries are small at the front and large at the back because most of the work happens where customers never go: the mixer, the long wooden bench, the proving racks, and an oven that takes up a whole wall.',
    'Smaller bakeries have started to change the hours again. Some bake twice a day, once for the morning rush and once for the evening, which spreads the work across the day and means the bread on the shelf at five is nearly as good as the bread at seven.',
    'Some things have not moved. The first batch still goes in before the street lights go off, and the best loaf of the day is still the one sold at seven to someone in work boots who did not ask what kind it was.',
    'Next time the bakery smells of toast at half past six, it is worth remembering that the smell is the end of somebody’s shift.'] },
  { id: 'a4', pub: 'Common Ground', by: 'Ade Bello', date: '21 Sep', saved: 6, tags: ['cities', 'design'], prog: 1, fav: true, hl: [1], title: 'A bench is a promise',
    dek: 'Where a city lets you sit down says a lot about who it was built for.', p: [
    'A city that puts a bench on a street is saying something: you can stop here, you are allowed to stay. Take the bench away and the message changes. Keep moving.',
    'Good benches are placed where people already pause. At the top of a hill, outside the pharmacy, beside the bus stop that runs every twenty minutes. Planners who watch before they draw get these right; the ones who work only from a map put benches facing a wall.',
    'The details matter more than the design. Armrests help older people stand up. A back makes it a place to rest rather than perch. Wood stays warmer than metal in winter, and a little shade decides whether anyone sits there in August.',
    'Benches are cheap compared with almost everything else on a street. That is exactly why they are easy to forget, and why a neighbourhood with plenty of them feels, without anyone quite noticing, like it was built for people.'] },
  { id: 'a5', pub: 'Plain Text Weekly', by: 'Jun Sato', date: '19 Sep', saved: 8, tags: ['work', 'design'], prog: .18, hl: [3], title: 'The case for boring software',
    dek: 'The systems that pay people and ship parcels are mostly dull. That is a compliment.', p: [
    'The software that runs the world is mostly dull. Payroll systems written decades ago still pay people on Friday. Databases nobody mentions at conferences hold every booking, every prescription, every parcel.',
    'Boring is a compliment. It means the tool has been used enough that its surprises have been found and fixed. When a team picks a well-worn database over a new one, it is choosing to spend its curiosity on the product rather than the plumbing.',
    'There is a cost. Boring tools can be slow to learn and old-fashioned to use, and they rarely make anyone’s portfolio sparkle. But the cost is paid once, up front, instead of at three in the morning when something novel fails in a novel way.',
    'Choosing the dull option is also a kindness to whoever comes next. The engineer who joins in two years will find answers in old forum threads and well-thumbed manuals, rather than in the head of the one person who picked the exciting thing and has since moved on.',
    'None of this is an argument against new ideas. It is an argument for spending them carefully, the way a cook spends salt: enough to matter, never so much that it is all you can taste.',
    'A useful rule: allow one new, interesting thing per project, and make everything around it as plain as possible. You will learn more from the one experiment when it is not competing with five others.',
    'The best compliment a system can get is that nobody remembers it is there.'] },
  { id: 'a6', pub: 'Shoreline', by: 'Ellis Navarro', date: '17 Sep', saved: 10, tags: ['science'], title: 'What tide pools teach about patience',
    dek: 'At low tide the rock shelf fills with small, still worlds that only show themselves to people who wait.', p: [
    'At low tide the rock shelf fills with small, still worlds. For the first minute you see nothing but water and weed. Then a shell walks, a shrimp flicks, and an anemone that looked like a stain opens into a flower.',
    'Tide pools reward the person who crouches and waits. Everything in them has learned to hide from gulls and sun, so the only way to see it is to become uninteresting: still, quiet, and in no hurry.',
    'The pools change twice a day. What was trapped this morning is gone by evening and something else has arrived. Coming back to the same pool for a week is like rereading a short book and finding new sentences each time.',
    'It is a decent model for attention in general. Most things worth noticing are small and slow, and they show themselves only after we have stopped looking for something bigger.'] },
  { id: 'a7', pub: 'The Ledger Review', by: 'Corin Hale', date: '9 Sep', saved: 21, tags: ['design'], prog: 1, archived: true, hl: [0], title: 'Letters nobody sends anymore',
    dek: 'The thank-you note survived the telephone and the email. It may not survive the emoji.', p: [
    'A handwritten note takes four minutes to write and is kept for twenty years. Few things we do have a better ratio of effort to effect.',
    'The format is simple: say what they did, say what it meant, say something about the future. Three sentences is plenty. The stamp is the hardest part.',
    'People who still send them do it for the same reason people still bake bread at home. It is slower, and the slowness is the point.'] },
  { id: 'a8', pub: 'Common Ground', by: 'Tamsin Reyes', date: '2 Sep', saved: 28, tags: ['cities'], prog: 1, archived: true, title: 'Maps drawn from memory',
    dek: 'Ask people to draw their neighbourhood and the streets they love grow wider.', p: [
    'When people draw their neighbourhood from memory, the map tells you less about the streets than about the person. Favourite routes grow wide; places they avoid shrink or vanish entirely.',
    'Planners have used these sketches for decades to find the edges people feel but no map shows: the road that is hard to cross, the underpass nobody likes, the park that seems further away than it is.'] },
];
